      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mount Olive was founded in 1846 by German immigrant John Niemann.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://mythicmississippi.illinois.edu/coal/mt-olive/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from Olive Nieman’s German “Oelberg,” meaning “Olive mountain,” a nod to the Biblical Mount of Olives.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.theroute-66.com/mount-olive.html",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "When the railroad arrived, the station was renamed Drummond Station and later Mount Olive, anchoring the town’s growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.theroute-66.com/mount-olive.html",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Route 66 once cut through Mount Olive’s north and west sides, and the old northbound lanes are now abandoned relics.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.theroute-66.com/mount-olive.html",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Union Miners Cemetery in Mount Olive, listed on the National Register of Historic Places, tells the story of the town’s coal‑mining past.",
